<section
  id='case-petart'
  class='fullscreen-section case-study-section'
  data-title='Case Study: Pet Art With Heart — Web Development | Katja Turnsek'
  data-description='Real-world artist website case study focused on responsive front-end development, artwork-led presentation, performance, accessibility, and search visibility.'
  data-case='petart'
  aria-labelledby='case-petart-title'
>
  <div class='rows'>
    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-header'>
        <p class='case-eyebrow'>Web Development</p>
        <h1 id='case-petart-title'>Pet Art With Heart</h1>
        <p class='case-subtitle'>A lightweight business website that presents commissioned pet
          portraits with clarity and warmth.</p>

        <dl class='case-summary' aria-label='Project summary'>
          <div>
            <dt>Role</dt>
            <dd>Brand, UX/UI, content, and front-end development</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Scope</dt>
            <dd>Independent real-world business site</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Status</dt>
            <dd>Ongoing</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Tools</dt>
            <dd>HTML, CSS, responsive images</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Outcome</dt>
            <dd>Live commission portfolio</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>

        <div class='tech-demo-wrapper case-demo-wrapper'>
          <div class='demo-links' data-auto='demo' data-case='petart'></div>
        </div>

        <nav
          class='project-switcher'
          aria-label='Pet Art With Heart sections'
          data-auto='switcher'
          data-case='petart'
        ></nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>The brief</h2>
      <p>
        Pet Art With Heart needed a website that could work as both an art portfolio and a
        trustworthy introduction to commissioned work. The portraits had to remain the visual focus
        while visitors could quickly understand the artistic style, the personal nature of the
        service, and how to take the next step.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-grid'>
        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--challenge'>
          <h2>Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            Detailed artwork benefits from large imagery, but heavy pages would work against
            discoverability and mobile browsing. The challenge was to create an expressive,
            image-led presentation that still loads efficiently, reads clearly, and supports the
            practical purpose of a commission website.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Let the portraits lead without hiding essential information</li>
            <li>Balance image quality with responsive performance</li>
            <li>Build a clear path from discovering the work to making contact</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--desktop'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/site-petart-600.webp'
            data-src='site-petart-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='400'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Pet Art With Heart desktop homepage presenting commissioned pet portraits and introductory content'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>Artwork-led desktop homepage</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--decisions'>
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Used semantic HTML and a clear content hierarchy so the artwork, service information,
              and calls to action remain understandable without scripting
            </li>
            <li>
              Built the layout with custom responsive CSS to retain precise control over image
              presentation, typography, and whitespace
            </li>
            <li>
              Kept decorative movement lightweight and CSS-based so it adds warmth without competing
              with the portraits or delaying the page
            </li>
            <li>
              Structured page content and metadata around the actual commission service to support
              relevant search visibility as well as human readers
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--mobile'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/site-petartmobile-600.webp'
            data-src='site-petartmobile-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='515'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Pet Art With Heart mobile homepage and navigation presenting pet portrait artwork'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>Mobile homepage and navigation</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row case-outcome'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>Outcome and reflection</h2>
      <p>
        The result is a live business site that connects my artwork, visual identity, service
        information, and contact journey in one responsive experience. Unlike a fictional brief,
        this project required me to make practical decisions for my own audience and balance
        artistic expression with performance, discoverability, and trust.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='back-to-work-container'>
      <a
        href='{{base}}#work'
        class='label-link back-to-work-label'
        data-back
        data-back-fallback='{{base}}#work'
        aria-label='Back to Work'
      >
        <svg class='icon icon-18 arrow-icon' aria-hidden='true'><use href='#i-arrow-left' /></svg>
        <span class='label-text'>Back to Work</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>